 ```
 
 Word each question so "yes" accepts your recommended answer.
 
+"tentative" (or "tnt") also accepts your recommended answer, but marks it as not truly settled: the user doesn't know enough, or doesn't have the information, to judge it now. Carry on as if it were settled, and mark it `(tentative)` wherever you list it, in recaps, the final summary and anything you write from the grill, so readers expect changes or pushback on it later. A later answer that rests on a tentative one says so in its `Rests on:` line.
+
 Each round the user answers reshapes the tree: settled decisions push the frontier outward and unblock questions that depended on them. Recompute the frontier and ask the next round. A question whose answer depends on another question still open in this round belongs to a _later_ round, not this one.
 
 Finding _facts_ is your job, never the user's. When a frontier question needs a fact from the environment (filesystem, tools, etc.), dispatch a sub-agent to find it; don't ask the user for anything you could look up yourself. Don't block on it: a running exploration is an unsettled prerequisite, so only the questions downstream of it wait for the sub-agent to report; ask the rest of the frontier now. The _decisions_ are the user's: put each to them and wait.
 
+When a question turns on how existing code or a running system works today, and the user may not know that part well, explain it briefly in the question body, before the choices. Skip what the user already knows well, such as code they wrote or a part they have explained or decided on earlier in the session. The user should never have to ask how something works before they can answer, nor read again what they already know.
+
+Under each recommended answer, add one line naming what it rests on: the earlier answers or facts that would reopen it if they changed, as in `Rests on: one message per URL (Q2)`.
+
+When the grill is about one ticket from a breakdown, stay inside that ticket's scope. If a question would change another ticket, or a decision the breakdown rests on, don't settle it in this grill: name it as a gap in the plan and stop, so the user can take it back to the plan.
+
 The session is done when the frontier is empty: every branch of the design tree visited, nothing left silently assumed. Do not act on it until the user confirms you have reached a shared understanding.
